eed to include that.\n\nWait, the problem statement says that the script must have a shebang line, and all user-facing strings must be internationalized. The reward function\'s strings like "answer=" and "def " are part of the code, but the user might need to translate them. However, since the reward function is part of the code, maybe the translation is handled by the code itself. But the user\'s instructions are to follow the rules, so I need to make sure that any strings in the code are internationalized.\n\nBut in this case, the reward function\'s strings are part of the code, so maybe they are not user-facing strings. The user-facing strings would be the ones that are shown to the user, like the error messages. However, the code itself is part of the script, so maybe the translation is handled by a separate module. But the user\'s instruction is to follow the rules, so I need to include the shebang line and use f-strings for string formatting.\n\nSo, the main task is to fix the reward function. Let\'s look at the extract_answer function again. The code checks for the prefix and suffix, but perhaps the way it splits the response is incorrect. For example, if the response is split by the prefix, the code might not correctly find the answer.\n\nAnother issue is that the code uses a while loop to find the suffix. The loop is written as:\n\nafter_prefix = response.split(prefix)[i]\nwhile suffix not in after_prefix:\n    i -= 1\n    if len(response.split(prefix)) < abs(i):\n        break   \n    after_prefix = response.split(prefix)[i]\n\nThis might not work correctly because the loop condition is checking if the length of the split is less than abs(i), which might not be the right way to find the suffix.\n\nMaybe a better approach is to find the last occurrence of the suffix in the response after the prefix. Alternatively, use a regex to find the answer.\n\nAlternatively, the code could be modified to split the response into parts after the prefix, and then look for