ring, then it\'s impossible to arrange without duplicates.\n\nSo, first step: count the frequency of each character.\n\nOnce that\'s done, check if the maximum frequency is more than (n + 1) // 2, where n is the length of the string. If yes, return None.\n\nOtherwise, proceed to rearrange the characters.\n\nNow, how to rearrange them. One way is to sort the characters and then arrange them in a way that no two same characters are adjacent.\n\nFor example, sorted list, then alternate characters. But how to do that in code.\n\nLet\'s think of the steps:\n\n- Sort the characters in non-decreasing order.\n- Then, iterate through the sorted list, placing each character in the result string, ensuring that the previous character is not the same.\n\nBut how to do that in a loop.\n\nAlternatively, we can use a greedy approach: take the first character, then the next character that\'s not the same as the previous, and so on.\n\nBut how to implement this in code with a loop.\n\nLet me think of the steps for the rearrangement:\n\n1. Get the sorted list of characters.\n\n2. Initialize a result list.\n\n3. Iterate through the sorted list, and for each character, add it to the result list, but only if it\'s not the same as the last character in the result.\n\nBut if the sorted list is [a, a, b, b], then the first a is added, then the next a is not allowed, so we take the next b, then the next b is not allowed, so we take the next a. But since the list is sorted, maybe we can alternate.\n\nWait, but this approach might not work for all cases. For example, if the sorted list is [a, a, a, b, b], then the first a is added, then the next a is not allowed, so we take the next a (but that\'s same as previous), so we can\'t. So, in this case, the function should return None.\n\nBut the problem is, when the sorted list has multiple same characters, how to arrange them.\n\nAnother approach is to use a backtracking method, but that\'s not efficient. However, for the sake of solving the probl