inputs, which the code does.\n\nSo the final code would be in the solution.py file as:\n\ndef check_isosceles(a, b, c):\n    return a == b or a == c or b == c\n\nI should also check if there are any edge cases. For example, if one of the inputs is zero? But the problem says the inputs are integers representing lengths, so perhaps zero is allowed. But the function would still work as intended. For example, 0,0,1 would return True.\n\nTherefore, the code should be correct.\n</think>\n\n```python\n# experiments/solution.py\n\ndef check_isosceles(a, b, c):\n    return a == b or a == c or b == c\n```'
