ke "abc", "123a", etc. Then converting to int would fail, so the function would return False. That\'s correct.\n\nSo the code for the function is straightforward. The user mentioned not to define any helper functions, so the solution should be a single function in the solution.py file.\n\nThe user also wants the code to be written as a linear script from top to bottom, but since the problem is just a function, the code can be placed in a single file. The run.sh script is for running the training, but the solution.py is the output file.\n\nSo the final code would be in experiments/solution.py, with the check_integer function defined.\n\nI need to make sure that the function is correctly implemented. Let me test some cases:\n\nTest case 1: "123" → True\nTest case 2: "123a" → False\nTest case 3: "  123 " → False (since leading/trailing spaces make it invalid)\nTest case 4: "0" → True\nTest case 5: "" → False (empty string)\nTest case 6: "123.45" → False (not integer)\nTest case 7: "123.0" → False (not integer)\n\nSo the function should handle these cases correctly.\n\nTherefore, the code for experiments/solution.py would be:\n\ndef check_integer(s):\n    try:\n        int(s)\n        return True\n    except ValueError:\n        return False\n\nThat\'s the solution.\n</think>\n\nexperiments/solution.py\n```python\ndef check_integer(s):\n    try:\n        int(s)\n        return True\n    except ValueError:\n        return False\n```'
