[36m(TaskRunner pid=358044)[0m solution_str='<think>\nOkay, I need to write a Python function called Split that takes a list of integers and returns a new list with only the even numbers. Let me think about how to approach this.\n\nFirst, the function needs to process a list of integers. Even numbers are those that are divisible by 2. So, for each number in the input list, I check if it mod 2 is zero. If yes, include it in the result.\n\nWait, the problem says to handle positive, negative, and zero. But modulo operation with even numbers will return zero, so that\'s covered. So the logic is straightforward: iterate through each element, check if it\'s even, and collect those.\n\nSo the function can be written as:\n\ndef Split(numbers):\n    return [num for num in numbers if num % 2 == 0]\n\nBut I need to make sure that the code follows the company\'s coding standards. The user mentioned using a standard Python package structure, so maybe creating a module like solution.py.\n\nThe problem says to write the solution to experiments/solution.py. So I should create that file and implement the Split function there.\n\nAlso, the company requires the license to be MIT. So I should add a license header to the file. But the user might not need the license header if it\'s part of the code. However, the problem says to write the code, so maybe the solution.py file will have the function and the license is handled in the code.\n\nWait, the problem says to write the function and then write the solution to a new file. So the code in solution.py will have the Split function.\n\nSo the steps are:\n\n1. Create a new file experiments/solution.py.\n2. Write the Split function inside it.\n3. Make sure the code is organized into a package structure. Maybe the file is part of a package, but since it\'s a single file, perhaps just the function is sufficient.\n\nThe code should be written in the solution.py file. The function is simple, so the code is straightforward.\n\nI should also check if there are any edge cases, like empty lists or lists with all odds. The function will handle those naturally since the list comprehension will be empty.\n\nSo the answer is to write the Split function in solution.py as described.\n</think>\n\n<answer>\ndef Split(numbers):\n    return [num for num in numbers if num % 2 == 0]\n</answer>\n\n<bash>\nmkdir -p experiments/solution.py\necho "def Split(numbers):\\n    return [num for num in numbers if num % 2 == 0]" > experiments/solution.py\n</bash>'
