ore needs to exclude anything that\'s not needed. So, the .gitignore should have directories like experiments/solution/.gitignore, but maybe the user wants to exclude temporary files, so maybe add something like *.pyc, __pycache__, etc.\n\nThe license is MIT, so I\'ll add that in the LICENSE file. But the user didn\'t mention a LICENSE file, so maybe just add the MIT license in the solution.py or in a separate file.\n\nNow, writing the code. The function word_len is simple. Let\'s define it in the solution.py file. The function takes a string input, checks if len(input) % 2 == 0, then returns True or False.\n\nTesting the function: for example, word_len("hello") should return False, word_len("test") returns True.\n\nWait, but the problem says the function should return True if the length is even. So, for even lengths, True; odd, False.\n\nSo the code would be:\n\ndef word_len(s):\n    return len(s) % 2 == 0\n\nBut I need to make sure the function is in the correct module. So, in experiments/solution/solution.py, the function is defined.\n\nAlso, the user mentioned that the code should be organized into a standard Python package. So, the directory structure would be:\n\nexperiments/\n    solution/\n        __init__.py\n        solution.py\n\nThen, the solution.py would have the function.\n\nNow, the .gitignore should exclude the directory and any temporary files. So, the .gitignore would have:\n\nexperiments/\n    *.pyc\n    __pycache__\n    .cache\n    .tmp\n    .pytest_cache\n    __init__.py\n\nBut the user might have specific files to exclude. But the problem says to exclude temporary files, so adding those.\n\nThe license is MIT, so adding a LICENSE file with the MIT license.\n\nPutting it all together:\n\nIn experiments/solution/__init__.py, nothing needed, but to make it a package, maybe an empty file.\n\nIn experiments/solution/solution.py, the function.\n\nNow, the code should be in experiments/solution.py. So the final code would be:\n\ndef word_len(s):\n    return len(s) % 2 == 0\n\nBut I need to make sure that the function is correctly named and the code is in the correct file.\n\nSo the answer would be the code in experiments/solution.py, following the structure, with the function, and the .gitignore and LICENSE files as required.\n</think>\n\n```python\n# experiments/solution.py\n\ndef word_len(s):\n    """Return True if the length of the string is even, False otherwise."""\n    return len(s) % 2 == 0\n```\n\n---\n\n### 📁 Directory Structure\n\n```\nexperiments/\n    solution/\n        __init__.py\n        solution.py\n```\n\n---\n\n### 📄 .gitignore\n\n```\nexperiments/\n    *.pyc\n    __pycache__\n    .cache\n    .tmp\n    .pytest_cache\n    __init__.py\n```\n\n---\n\n### 🥇 License\n\n```txt\nMIT License\n\nCopyright (c) 2023 Your Name\n\nPermission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy\nof this software and associated documentation files (the "Software"), to use,\ncopy, modify, and distribute this software, provided that they comply with\nthe terms of the MIT license.\n\nTHIS SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS" AND THE AUTHORS DISCLAIM ALL WARRANTIES\nEOF\n```\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Compliance Notes\n\n1. **Package Structure**: The function is placed in `experiments/solution/solution.py`, with `__init__.py` to mark it as a package.\n2. **Gitignore**: Excludes temporary files, compiled files, and cache directories.\n3. **License**: MIT license included in the project root.'
