emented. Let me check for some edge cases. What if the string is empty? Then the function returns an empty string. What if the string has only one character? Then it returns the character at index 1, which is out of bounds. Wait, but in that case, len(s) is 1, so range(1, 1, 2) is an empty list. So the result is empty. Which is correct because there are no even indices to remove. So the function would return an empty string.\n\nAnother test case: input "a", output is empty. Input "abc", output is "bc". Input "abcd", output is "cd". That seems correct.\n\nNow, the problem says that constants should be in uppercase with underscores. But the function name is remove_even, which is already in lowercase. So that\'s okay. The function doesn\'t have any constants, so no issues there.\n\nThe solution needs to be in a file called solution.py. So I need to write the function in that file. Also, the problem says to avoid third-party libraries, which the code does, since it\'s using Python\'s standard features.\n\nSo the code for solution.py would be:\n\ndef remove_even(s):\n    return \'\'.join([s[i] for i in range(1, len(s), 2)])\n\nBut wait, the problem says to put the solution in experiments/solution.py. So I need to create that file. Let me check the directory structure. The user has a directory experiments, and in it, there\'s a problem.txt and a run.sh. So the solution.py should be in the experiments directory.\n\nSo the steps are:\n\n1. Create a file called solution.py in the experiments directory.\n2. Write the function as described.\n\nBut the user might have existing files. The current directory is /workspace/qwen3_rl_training, and the experiments directory is inside. So I can create the solution.py file.\n\nSo the bash command would be:\n\necho "def remove_even(s): return \'\'.join([s[i] for i in range(1, len(s), 2)])" > experiments/solution.py\n\nBut I need to make sure that the file is saved with UTF-8 encoding. The echo command should handle that.\n\nAlternatively