nt(n**0.5) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            if i * i == n:\n                count += 1\n            else:\n                count += 2\n    return count\n\nBut the problem says to use a list comprehension. So maybe we can use a generator expression to count the divisors.\n\nWait, another approach. The list comprehension can generate all divisors, then take the length. But for that, we need to generate all divisors.\n\nBut generating all divisors can be done with a list comprehension. For example, for each i in 1 to n, check if i divides n, and collect them. Then the length of the list is the answer.\n\nBut that's O(n) time, which is acceptable for small n, but for large n, it's not efficient. However, the problem doesn't specify constraints on n, so maybe the solution is acceptable.\n\nBut the problem says to use a list comprehension and make it a single declarative expression. So perhaps the solution is to generate all divisors using a list comprehension and then return the length.\n\nSo, here's the code:\n\ndef divisor(n):\n    return len([i for i in range(1, n+1) if n % i == 0])\n\nBut wait, that's a list comprehension. However, for large n, this could be slow. But the problem doesn't mention any constraints, so it's acceptable.\n\nAlternatively, using a generator expression inside the len() function.\n\nSo, the code would be:\n\ndef divisor(n):\n    return len([i for i in range(1, n+1) if n % i == 0])\n\nBut the problem says to use a list comprehension. So this is acceptable.\n\nBut the user wants the solution in a file called experiments/solution.py. So the code would be written in that file.\n\nSo the answer would be the function divisor as described.\n\nBut the problem says that the solution should be a single, declarative expression. So the code would be written as a single line.\n\nWait, but the function is supposed to be written as a single expression. So perhaps:\n\ndef divisor(n):\n    return len([i for i in range(1, n+1) if n % i == 0])\n\nBut 