k if \'a\' is present, then \'e\', etc. But using the subset method is more concise and efficient.\n\nSo the code would be:\n\ndef check(s):\n    return \'accepted\' if set(\'aeiou\').issubset(set(s.lower())) else \'not accepted\'\n\nBut wait, the problem says "regardless of case", so converting the string to lowercase is correct. \n\nYes, this should work. The code is a single expression, concise, and follows the coding standards by avoiding functions or classes. It\'s efficient and minimal.\n</think>\n\n<answer>\ndef check(s):\n    return \'accepted\' if set(\'aeiou\').issubset(set(s.lower())) else \'not accepted\'\n</answer>'
