y and the length. But in the problem statement, the function is supposed to take the array and its length. So the parameters are arr and n.\n\nWait, the problem says "takes an array and its length as inputs". So the function is called with the array and the length. So the parameters are arr and n.\n\nSo the code would be:\n\ndef count_Pairs(arr, n):\n    freq = {}\n    for i in range(n):\n        x = arr[i]\n        if x in freq:\n            freq[x] += 1\n        else:\n            freq[x] = 1\n    total = 0\n    for x in freq.values():\n        total += x * (x - 1) // 2\n    return total\n\nBut the user wants the code to be as short as possible, using single-letter variables. Let me see if I can make this even shorter.\n\nFor example, using x instead of the variable name. But the code would need to be concise. Let\'s see.\n\nAlternatively, using a list comprehension or other methods. But for the sake of brevity, the code would need to be as short as possible.\n\nAnother approach is to use a generator expression. For example, the sum of (k*(k-1)//2) for each k in the frequency values.\n\nSo the code could be:\n\ndef count_Pairs(arr, n):\n    return sum(k * (k - 1) // 2 for k in [x.count() for x in [arr[i] for i in range(n)]])\n\nWait, but that\'s not correct. Because x.count() would be for each element, but that\'s not the way to get the frequencies. So maybe:\n\nWait, the code can be written as:\n\ndef count_Pairs(arr, n):\n    return sum(k * (k - 1) // 2 for k, _ in Counter(arr).items())\n\nBut Counter is from collections. However, the user\'s instructions say to avoid function calls overhead. But in Python, using Counter might be considered a function call. However, the user\'s example code uses a dictionary, so maybe it\'s acceptable.\n\nBut the problem is that the code must be as compact as possible. Let me think again.\n\nAlternatively, using a generator expression with a dictionary.\n\nBut the code must be as short as possible. Let me think of the most concise way.\n\nAnother way: use a loop to count frequencies and then compute the sum.\n\nBut in Python, for the purposes of brevity, perhaps the code can be written as:\n\ndef count_Pairs(arr, n):\n    return sum(x * (x - 1) // 2 for x in [1 + ...] ...)\n\nWait, but how to get the frequencies. Let me think of the code as:\n\ndef count_Pairs(arr, n):\n    from collections import Counter\n    return sum(k * (k - 1) // 2 for k in Counter(arr).values())\n\nBut the problem is that the code must be written without any extra imports, but the user\'s problem allows that. However, in the solution, I need to make sure that the code is as concise as possible.\n\nBut the user\'s instructions say that the code must be as compact and concise as possible, avoiding function call overhead. So using Counter might be considered a function call, but perhaps it\'s acceptable.\n\nBut maybe there\'s a way to do this without using Counter. For example, using a loop.\n\nBut the code must be as short as possible. Let me try to write it.\n\nAnother approach: using a loop to count the frequencies and then compute the sum.\n\nBut in Python, the code can be written as:\n\ndef count_Pairs(arr, n):\n    freq = {}\n    for i in range(n):\n        x = arr[i]\n        if x in freq:\n            freq[x] += 1\n        else:\n            freq[x] = 1\n    return sum(k * (k - 1) // 2 for k in freq.values())\n\nThis is the same as the initial code, but the variables are named as per the rules. The variable names are x, k, etc., which are single letters. So this code would meet the requirements.\n\nBut the problem is that the code is written in Python, and the function is named count_Pairs. The parameters are arr and n.\n\nSo the code would be:\n\ndef count_Pairs(arr, n):\n    freq = {}\n    for i in range(n):\n        x = arr[i]\n        if x in freq:\n            freq[x] += 1\n        else:\n            freq[x] = 1\n    return sum(k * (k - 1) // 2 for k in freq.values())\n\nBut in the problem, the function is