unction should take those parameters.\n\nSo the final code would be the function as described. No classes or functions defined, just the inline logic. The code is concise, uses single-letter variables (though in the example, the parameters are a, b, c, which are single letters, but the variables in the code are a, b, c. So that fits the requirement of single-letter variables.\n\nCheck if the code is performance-critical. Since it's a simple function, it's straightforward and doesn't have any overhead. So it meets the performance requirement.\n\nThus, the solution is the function as outlined.\n</think>\n\n<answer>\ndef median_numbers(a: int, b: int, c: int) -> float:\n    numbers = [a, b, c]\n    numbers.sort()\n    return float(numbers[1])\n</answer>"
