unter. However, the code is still using string conversion.\n\nBut the user\'s instructions are to use a standard for loop and avoid magic one-liners. So perhaps the for loop is acceptable.\n\nAlternatively, using the while loop with a counter. Let\'s think about that.\n\nAnother approach is to use a while loop with a counter:\n\ndef sum_digits(n):\n    if n < 0:\n        return 0\n    total = 0\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(str(n)):\n        digit = int(str(n)[i])\n        total += digit\n        i += 1\n    return total\n\nBut this is using the len(str(n)) to determine the number of digits, which is a standard approach.\n\nBut the problem says to avoid magic one-liners. So this code is acceptable.\n\nAlternatively, using the for loop with a range from 0 to the number of digits minus one.\n\nBut how to get the number of digits. For example:\n\nn = 123\ndigits = len(str(n))  # 3\nfor i in range(digits):\n    digit = int(str(n)[i])\n    total += digit\n\nThis is using the string conversion, which is acceptable.\n\nBut the user might have a preference for not using string conversion, but since the problem requires a function that sums the digits of a number, converting to a string is the most straightforward way.\n\nSo, the function can be written as:\n\ndef sum_digits(n):\n    if n < 0:\n        return 0\n    total = 0\n    for digit_str in str(n):\n        total += int(digit_str)\n    return total\n\nBut the user said to use a standard for loop and avoid magic one-liners. The code here uses a for loop, which is standard.\n\nAnother approach is to use a while loop with a counter:\n\ndef sum_digits(n):\n    if n < 0:\n        return 0\n    total = 0\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(str(n)):\n        digit = int(str(n)[i])\n        total += digit\n        i += 1\n    return total\n\nBut this is a while loop with a counter. However, the code is still using string conversion.\n\nBut the user\'s instruction is to use a standard for loop and avoid magic one-liners. The code is