ove.\n\nNow, check the code for possible issues. For example, when n=1, the loop runs from 2 to 1, which doesn\'t run, so result remains 1. Correct.\n\nAnother test case: n=5. The loop runs for i=2,3,4,5. Let\'s see:\n\nresult starts at 1.\n\ni=2: result = 1*2 / gcd(1,2) = 2/1=2.\n\ni=3: 2*3=6 / gcd(2,3)=1 → 6.\n\ni=4: 6*4=24 / gcd(6,4)=2 → 12.\n\ni=5: 12*5=60 / gcd(12,5)=1 → 60. Correct.\n\nYes.\n\nSo the code is correct.\n</think>\n\n<answer>\nimport math\n\ndef smallest_multiple(n):\n    result = 1\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        result = (result * i) // math.gcd(result, i)\n    return result\n</answer>'
