[36m(TaskRunner pid=358044)[0m solution_str='<think>\nOkay, let\'s tackle this problem. The user wants a function called check_integer that takes a string and returns True if it\'s an integer, else False. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out how to determine if a string is an integer. The string could be something like "123", "123a", or "-123". So, the function needs to check if all characters are digits and there\'s no leading or trailing whitespace.\n\nI remember that in Python, you can use the str.isdigit() method, but that might not work if the string has leading or trailing whitespace. Also, it should handle negative numbers. So, maybe the approach is to check if the string is all digits, and then check if it\'s not empty and doesn\'t have any non-digit characters.\n\nWait, but what if the string is empty? The problem says it\'s a single string input, so maybe the function should handle empty strings as not integers. Also, numbers like "123.0" would be invalid because they have a decimal point. So the function should check for digits only, no decimals, and no leading/trailing spaces.\n\nSo the steps would be:\n1. Check if the string is empty. If so, return False.\n2. Check if all characters are digits. If not, return False.\n3. Check if there\'s any non-digit character. If there is, return False.\n4. Return True if all these conditions are met.\n\nBut how to implement this in Python. Let\'s think. The string can be checked using the isdigit() method. However, that\'s not sufficient because it might include characters like \'+\' or \'-\' at the start. So, perhaps using a regex would be better. The regex pattern for a valid integer is ^-?\\d+$, which allows optional leading minus sign and digits only.\n\nSo, the function can use a regex to match the string. If the regex matches, return True; else, False.\n\nAlternatively, check if the string is not empty, all characters are digits, and there\'s no leading or trailing whitespace. But that\'s more complicated. The regex approach is straightforward.\n\nSo the function would be something like:\n\ndef check_integer(s):\n    import re\n    return bool(re.fullmatch(r\'^-?\\d+$\', s))\n\nBut wait, what about leading or trailing whitespace? The regex ^-?\\d+$ will not match strings with spaces. So that\'s handled.\n\nTesting some cases:\n- "123" → True\n- "123a" → False\n- "-123" → True\n- "123 " → False\n- "" → False\n\nYes, that seems right. So the function would be implemented with a regex check. The code should be self-documenting, so the function name is clear, and the comment explains the approach.\n</think>\n\n<answer>\ndef check_integer(s):\n    import re\n    return bool(re.fullmatch(r\'^-?\\d+$\', s))\n</answer>'
