[36m(TaskRunner pid=358044)[0m solution_str='<think>\nOkay, let\'s tackle this problem. The user wants me to write a Python function called is_odd that checks if a number is odd using bitwise operations. The function needs to take an integer num and return True or False. \n\nFirst, I remember that in Python, the modulo operator % can be used to check if a number is odd. For example, if num % 2 is 1, then it\'s odd. But the problem specifies using bitwise operations, so I need to find a way to do this without using the % operator.\n\nBitwise operations involve the binary representation of numbers. The least significant bit (LSB) determines the parity of the number. If the LSB is 1, the number is odd; if 0, it\'s even. So, checking the LSB of the number would give the answer.\n\nIn Python, the bitwise AND operator & can be used. If I do num & 1, that should give 1 if the number is odd and 0 if even. So the function could be as simple as returning whether num & 1 is 1.\n\nWait, but the problem says to use bitwise operations. So using num & 1 is a valid approach here. But I need to make sure that the function is correctly implemented.\n\nLet me think about the function signature. The user specified that the function should be def is_odd(num: int) -> bool: So the function takes an integer and returns a boolean.\n\nSo the code would be:\n\ndef is_odd(num: int) -> bool:\n    """Check if the number is odd using bitwise operations.\n    \n    Args:\n        num: An integer to check.\n        \n    Returns:\n        True if the number is odd, False otherwise.\n    """\n    return num & 1 != 0\n\nWait, but in Python, if num is even, num & 1 would be 0, so return False. If odd, 1, return True. So the condition is correct.\n\nBut I need to add inline comments as per the rules. Also, the function must have a docstring explaining its purpose and parameters.\n\nSo the docstring would describe what the function does, the parameter, and the return value.\n\nAnother thing to consider: the user mentioned that the function should use bitwise operations, so using num & 1 is the right approach here.\n\nI should also make sure that the function is properly formatted with the required docstring and comments. Let me check the rules again:\n\n- Every function must have a complete docstring explaining purpose and parameters.\n- Add inline comments for non-obvious steps.\n- Use verbose variable names.\n\nIn this case, the function is straightforward, so the docstring is clear. The inline comment might be redundant, but the docstring already explains it.\n\nSo the final code would be the function with the docstring and the return statement.\n</think>\n\n<answer>\ndef is_odd(num: int) -> bool:\n    """\n    Check if the number is odd using bitwise operations.\n    \n    Args:\n        num: An integer to check.\n        \n    Returns:\n        True if the number is odd, False otherwise.\n    """\n    # Use bitwise AND with 1 to check if the least significant bit is 1 (odd)\n    return num & 1 != 0\n\n</answer>\n\n<bash>\n# Create the solution.py file in the experiments directory\necho "def is_odd(num: int) -> bool:\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    Check if the number is odd using bitwise operations.\\n\\n    Args:\\n        num: An integer to check.\\n\\n    Returns:\\n        True if the number is odd, False otherwise.\\n    \\"\\"\\"\\n    return num & 1 != 0" > experiments/solution.py\n</bash>'
